scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Use `AbortController` in the effect and ignore aborted responses. Alternatively, use a request token/sequence to discard out-of-date responses. Example with abort:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  const controller = new AbortController();
  setLoading(true);
  fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(q)}`, { signal: controller.signal })
    .then(r => r.json())
    .then(data => setResults(data))
    .catch(err => { if (err.name !== 'AbortError') throw err; })
    .finally(() => setLoading(false));
  return () => controller.abort();
}, [q]);
```
This ties each fetch to the effect invocation for a specific `q`. When `q` changes, cleanup runs, aborting the in-flight fetch so its resolution can't clobber state. A sequence counter variant:

```jsx
useEffect(() => {
  let alive = true; const seq = ++lastSeqRef.current;
  setLoading(true);
  fetch(url).then(r => r.json()).then(d => { if (alive && seq === lastSeqRef.current) setResults(d); })
    .finally(() => alive && setLoading(false));
  return () => { alive = false; };
}, [q]);
```
Both ensure only the latest request updates state.

Define nested routes under `/onboard` with explicit step paths: `/onboard/step-1`, `/onboard/step-2`, `/onboard/step-3`, and a catch-all redirect to step-1. Maintain a single source of truth for form data in a parent `<OnboardLayout>` that renders an `<Outlet/>` and provides `[data, setData]` and validation/helpers via context or `Outlet` context. Each step reads/writes via that shared API, so browser navigation never discards state. Gate Next by local validation: compute `isValid` in the step from its slice (or centralize with a validator that accepts the slice), and disable Next until true; on Next, use `useNavigate()` to push the next route. Example:

```jsx
function OnboardLayout() {
  const [data, setData] = useState({ name:'', email:'', plan:'' });
  return <OnboardContext.Provider value={{ data, setData }}><Outlet /></OnboardContext.Provider>;
}

function Step1() {
  const { data, setData } = useContext(OnboardContext);
  const valid = data.name.trim().length >= 2;
  return (
    <>
      <input value={data.name} onChange={e => setData(d => ({ ...d, name: e.target.value }))} />
      <button disabled={!valid} onClick={() => navigate('../step-2')}>Next</button>
    </>
  );
}
```
Deep-linking works because loading `/onboard/step-2` renders `<OnboardLayout>` first (creating state) then `<Step2>`. State persists across steps since it lives above them. Validation is per-step and only gates navigation from that step. Back/forward uses URLs; state remains intact until leaving the flow or unmounting the layout.

