scenario_questions.scenario:
Intermittently, your `POST /orders` returns `200 OK` with JSON, but logs show `Error: Can't set headers after they are sent.` Code excerpt:

```javascript
app.use(express.json());
app.use((req, res, next) => { req.start = Date.now(); next(); });

app.post('/orders', async (req, res, next) => {
  const doc = await db.collection('orders').insertOne(req.body);
  res.status(201).json({ id: doc.insertedId });
  next(); // trace middleware expects to run
});

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  console.log('Trace', req.method, req.url, Date.now() - req.start);
  res.set('X-Trace', 'ok'); // sometimes throws
  next();
});

app.use((err, req, res, next) => res.status(500).json({ error: 'boom' }));
```
Traffic includes some proxies that retry on 5xx.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
- The route sends a 201 response, then calls `next()`. Downstream middleware attempts to mutate headers (`res.set`) after the response has been committed, triggering `Can't set headers after they are sent.` Under some timings it may not throw (if not yet flushed), hence intermittent.
- Fix: Do not call `next()` after sending a response. Either (a) move tracing ahead of the route (pre-handler), recording start time and register `on('finish')` to log when the response ends, or (b) implement trace as terminal middleware that only observes `res` events and does not attempt to write headers.
- Example pattern:

```javascript
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  req.start = Date.now();
  res.on('finish', () => {
    console.log('Trace', req.method, req.url, Date.now() - req.start);
  });
  next();
});

app.post('/orders', async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const doc = await db.collection('orders').insertOne(req.body);
    res.status(201).set('X-Trace', 'ok').json({ id: doc.insertedId });
  } catch (e) {
    next(e);
  }
});
```
- Ensure error-handling middleware stays last. Avoid any middleware writing to the response after a handler has sent it.

